  "caption": "Sound does not fade. It jumps.\nScience 393, 1217-1220, 17 Sept 2026, DOI 10.1126/science.aeh7535. Makihara, Szakiel, Safavi-Naeini and colleagues, Stanford.\n\nStanford physicists (Safavi-Naeini lab) directly watched single phonons, the quantum packets of vibration, jump between energy levels in a mechanical resonator in real time. Quantum jumps: ions 1986, photons 2007, sound 2026. Science, 17 Sept 2026, DOI 10.1126/science.aeh7535.\n\n#physics #quantum #science #stanford",
  "voiceover_script": "Sound does not fade away smoothly. At the smallest scale, it jumps. Sound is vibration, and vibration is energy. At the quantum level that energy comes in packets called phonons. A phonon can only sit on fixed energy steps, never in between. Physicists at Stanford, in the Safavi-Naeini lab, built a tiny mechanical resonator that rings for about two milliseconds, and watched single phonons jump between those steps in real time. It is the first direct real-time observation of a quantum jump in sound. Quantum jumps were seen in ions in 1986, in photons in 2007, and now in sound.",
